From: "Barak A. Pearlmutter" <barak+git@pearlmutter.net>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 16:35:14 +0100
Subject: GUI: page offset, with per-file page stepping

When material added to one document shifts the pages of the other, the
page ranges had to be edited, the files compared again, and the
relevant pair found each time.  Instead:

- An Offset spin box (Controls dock) pairs page i of file #1's page
  range with page i + offset of file #2's.  It is 0 by default, giving
  the old pairing, and resets to 0 when either file changes.
- Small arrow buttons at the right of each file's page range show the
  previous/next page of that file beside the same page of the other,
  changing the offset by one.
- Changing the offset redoes the comparison immediately; thanks to the
  fingerprint cache this takes a few milliseconds, and only pages not
  yet examined are processed.
- The pair being looked at stays in view even if it does not differ:
  the View combobox then shows "(Not viewing)", the status says the
  pages appear the same, no "False Positive" label is drawn, and
  Previous/Next go to the nearest differing pair before/after it.

To support viewing any pair, MainWindow records each comparison's page
lists, offset and per-pair results, and tracks the viewed pair itself
(viewedPairIndex) instead of reading it from the View combobox;
showZones() uses it too.  PagePair gains a "differs" flag, and the
pixmap cache key is no longer tied to the combobox index.  Batch mode,
which has --startPage1 and --startPage2, is unchanged.  The help
describes the offset.

Tested with a program that drives MainWindow offscreen on a 10-page file
and a copy with a page inserted after page 2: stepping file #2 on at
"3 vs. 3" gives offset 1, shows 3 vs. 4 as the same, and leaves only
1 vs. 2 and 2 vs. 3 differing; Previous, stepping either file, and
typing offsets behave as expected, each step taking a few milliseconds.

