+<h3 id="off">Page Offset</h3>
+<p>The <b>Offset</b> pairs each page of the first PDF with the page that
+many pages further on in the second PDF (or further back, if the offset
+is negative), counting pages within the page ranges. For example, with
+an offset of 1 the pages are compared in the pairs (1, 2), (2, 3), and
+so on. This is useful when material added to one PDF shifts the pages
+of the other, so that, for example, everything after a certain point is
+a page later.
+
+<p>The small arrow buttons at the right of each PDF's page range show
+the previous or next page of that PDF beside the same page of the
+other, changing the offset by one. So when added material puts one PDF
+out of step, step it on to bring the pages that correspond back
+together, without editing the page ranges. The comparison is redone
+straight away, and the pair shown is shown whether or not it differs.
+
+<p>Pages already examined are remembered, so changing the offset, or
+comparing again, is quick.
+
 <h3 id="mar">Margins</h3>
 <p>It is possible to make <font color="darkgreen">disparPDF</font> ignore
 any text that is above a specified top margin, below a specified bottom
